## Architecture

### Component Hierarchy

```
<TerminalContainer>
  ├── <TerminalTopBar />           (existing — settings, fullscreen, mode toggle)
  ├── <EnhancedTerminalView />     (new — React block renderer)
  │   ├── <AgentMessageBlock />
  │   ├── <ToolCallBlock />
  │   ├── <ToolOutputBlock />
  │   ├── <UserInputBlock />
  │   ├── <StatusBarBlock />
  │   └── <PlainBlock />           (fallback for unclassified lines)
  ├── <RawTerminalView />          (existing — xterm.js canvas, hidden in enhanced mode)
  └── <HiddenTerminal />           (new — headless xterm.js for state tracking in enhanced mode)
```

### Data Flow

```
1. PTY data arrives via WebSocket
2. Data is written to the hidden xterm.js instance (state machine)
3. On every render cycle (requestAnimationFrame or 200ms interval):
   a. Read the visible viewport from xterm.js buffer
   b. For each line, read text + cell attributes (color, bold, bg, indent)
   c. Group consecutive lines into blocks based on attribute transitions
   d. Classify each block (agent-text, tool-call, user-input, etc.)
   e. Render blocks as React components
4. User input is captured by a custom input component and sent to PTY via WebSocket
5. If classification confidence is low for a region, render those lines as PlainBlock (monospace, unstyled)
```

### Toggle Behavior

| Mode | What renders | xterm.js role |
|------|-------------|---------------|
| Raw | Visible xterm.js canvas (current behavior) | Visible, receives PTY data directly |
| Enhanced | React block components | Hidden, headless state tracker only |

Switching modes is instant — no reconnection, no data loss. Both modes share the same WebSocket and PTY session.

