Hitting this regularly in real use — wanted to add some observations from running ~50 spawns/week on a BriteCore-internal agent harness.

The code has changed since the issue was filed. `session-manager.ts` now does an exponential-backoff retry loop (3s, 6s, 9s — `baseDelayMs * attempt`) instead of the fixed 5s sleep:

```ts
for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, baseDelayMs * attempt));
  await plugins.runtime.sendMessage(handle, agentLaunchConfig.prompt);
  promptDelivered = true;
  break;
}
```

The catch is that the retry doesn't actually help in our setup. `runtime.sendMessage` ends up calling `tmux send-keys`, which always returns success — it types the characters whether or not Claude Code is ready to consume them. So the `try { ... } catch { retry }` never triggers; Composio always reports "delivered ✓" on the first attempt.

What we see on the user side: ~60% of spawns leave Claude Code with the prompt visible in the pane but `0 tokens` and `Idle`. The text was typed, but the Enter arrived during a render frame and got dropped. Sending any single character through `ao send <id> "begin"` flushes the buffer and the agent picks up the previously-typed prompt and runs.

So the original suggestion to poll for the agent's ready state before sending is still right, but it'd be even better paired with verification *after* the send — e.g. tail Claude Code's JSONL for a new `user` entry within N seconds of `sendMessage` returning, and retry if it never appears. That closes the loop on the receive side, not just the send side.

